Package: homelab-edge-forward
Architecture: all
Depends: perl (>= 5.36), libyaml-libyaml-perl, homelab-common (>= 0.1.19), nftables
Recommends: homelab-agent
Description: DNAT+SNAT port-forwarding from a public IP to an internal backend
 Ships homelab-edge-forward-apply, which reads an editable
 /etc/homelab/edge-forward/forwards.yml (public port -> backend
 host:port, one or more L4 protocols each) and regenerates a
 self-contained /etc/nftables.d/50-edge-forward.conf owning its own
 `table ip nat` (real DNAT+SNAT, not plain interface forwarding --
 the backend has no public IP of its own, so without SNAT its replies
 would route out its own default gateway and never reach the real
 client). Validates with `nft -c -f` (which follows the resulting
 `include` directive itself) before ever touching the live ruleset.
 .
 Exists for the same reason homelab-haproxy does -- decoupling "holds
 the public IP" from "runs the actual service" -- but for protocols
 HAProxy structurally can't front (UDP; see homelab-haproxy's own
 Description). The first real user is PowerDNS: keeping authoritative
 DNS off the same host as the public-facing HAProxy/webproxy edge
 (separate blast radius for DNS-specific risks like UDP
 amplification), while its public port keeps answering from the same
 IP the domain's NS records already delegate to.
 .
 Deliberately does NOT load-balance across multiple backends behind
 one forward: when real multi-nameserver redundancy is needed, an
 independent second host with its own public IP and its own NS record
 is DNS's own native mechanism for that, not something to build here.
 .
 Also idempotently adds a narrow accept rule to the EXISTING
 `inet filter` table's `chain forward` for each configured forward --
 unavoidable, since a new `ip nat` table's own chains don't bypass a
 different table's independently-hooked `forward` chain policy.
